  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "The function $x(t)={t}^{2}+t$ models the distance traveled by a car $t$ seconds after it sets off on a journey. Find the instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing with respect to time when $t=1$ second.",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{\u2032}(1).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $x(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)=2t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=1$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(1)=2(1)+1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing when $t=1$ second is $3$ meters per second.",
